 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S442-023\", \"clauses\": {\"present\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Make a picture graph to show: {data_str}. Each {symbol} equals {scale}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 has the learner make a pictograph with a scale from a data list, so it addresses the task Present. Its data_str is nested F21 rows and its scale comes from the repeated scale values.\"}, \"raw_data\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F17\", \"template\": \"Look at the data: {data_str}. Draw a picture graph where each {symbol} stands for {scale} items.\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 gives category-and-count entries as unorganized data (nested F21) and asks for a pictograph with a scale, which addresses raw data.\"}, \"data\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F14\", \"template\": \"Complete the picture graph to show: {data_str}. Each {symbol} represents {scale} items.\", \"reasoning\": \"F14 presents a data set in the stem and asks the learner to represent it in a pictograph with a scale.\"}, \"tabular_form\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F20\", \"template\": \"The table shows: {data_str}. Create a picture graph where each {symbol} equals {scale}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F20 presents the data as a table (nested F21 rows) and asks for a pictograph with a scale, which addresses tabular form.\"}, \"pictograph\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"Look at the picture graph. Each {symbol} = {scale}. {sub_q}\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 shows a pictograph with its scale key and asks a question about it, which addresses the pictograph representation.\"}, \"scale\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F07\", \"template\": \" The scale is {scale}: each symbol stands for {scale} items.\", \"reasoning\": \"F07 names the scale and states what each symbol stands for, which addresses the scale property.\"}, \"vice_versa\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F09\", \"template\": \"Look at the {pg_word}. Write the correct count for each row in the {table_word}: {cat_list}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F09 gives a pictograph and asks for a table of counts, which is the reverse direction from presenting data in a pictograph.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F04\", \"F12\", \"F14\", \"F15\", \"F16\", \"F17\", \"F18\", \"F19\", \"F20\", \"F21\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"For each category, divide the number of items by {scale} to find how many pictures to draw.\", \"{join} and {v}\", \"Complete the picture graph to show: {data_str}. Each {symbol} represents {scale} items.\", \"How many books were read on {cat}?\", \"How many pupils play {cat}?\", \"Look at the data: {data_str}. Draw a picture graph where each {symbol} stands for {scale} items.\", \"Look at the picture graph. Each {symbol} = {scale}. {sub_q}\", \"Make a picture graph to show: {data_str}. Each {symbol} equals {scale}.\", \"The table shows: {data_str}. Create a picture graph where each {symbol} equals {scale}.\", \"{v}: {v2}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"Count-versus-scale defect: the F21 v2 domain (the graph's counts: 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 20, 30) has no derivation from scale, so scale-10 and scale-5 problems can print counts that whole pictures cannot show (for example 4 items at scale 10 gives 0.4 pictures, 15 items at scale 10 gives 1.5). F19, F14, F17 and F20 print this through nested F21, and F04 divides the count by scale and so asks for a non-whole number of pictures. Theme-row defect: the F18 sub_q domain draws F15 and F16 questions whose cat is read from the same _THEMES row as the symbol, but F15 and F16 fix the word books or pupils and their subject is not tied to the theme, so a row whose symbol and categories are foods, colours or grades can produce a question about books read on that item or pupils playing that item. Duplicate and mixed-theme defect: F12 template {join} and {v} draws v from the table's categories over all rows, and v has no distinctness claim against p0, p1 and p2 (distinct_in_every_render applies only to the F13 slot group), so a category can repeat, and cat_list in F08, F09 and F10 can mix categories from different rows under one pictograph. Clauses are producible from the families listed, but the families that produce them fail Rule 1.\"}",
